from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q001-060')
T={
'sets':r'''A set is a collection of objects, and a subset relation means that every member of one collection belongs to another. The statement “all X are Y” is the inclusion X⊆Y; it does not assert Y⊆X. Inclusion is transitive: X⊆Y and Y⊆Z imply X⊆Z. Venn diagrams divide a union into disjoint membership regions, so each printed regional count is counted once in the total. An intersection of two sets may include their triple intersection; “exactly two” expressly excludes that central region. When interpreting an unlabelled diagram, compare containment and overlap relations, rather than the apparent sizes of the curves. Ordinary category meanings determine inclusion, while the drawing does not imply numerical cardinalities. A useful check is to supply an object in each visibly allowed region and see whether its proposed category memberships are consistent.''',
'ratio':r'''A ratio records relative quantities: a:b:c means ak,bk,ck for one common positive scale k. Percent means per hundred, so a percentage increase p replaces a quantity x by x(1+p/100). Fractions of fractions are multiplied, whereas successive additions and subtractions must use the stated reference quantity. Direct proportionality means y=kx with the same constant k across comparisons; this constant can be cancelled when ranking values. Percentage error is measured against the intended or original quantity, not automatically against the observed quantity. To solve a word problem, introduce the common scale, translate each change algebraically, and reduce the resulting ratio. State units throughout. Common mistakes include adding a fixed amount to ratio entries without determining their scale, confusing percentage points with percent change, and treating recovered losses as a fraction of the remaining balance.''',
'geometry':r'''Geometric calculations combine a formula with its dimensional meaning. A circle of radius r has circumference 2πr and area πr²; a cylinder of base radius R and height h has volume πR²h, and a sphere of radius a has volume 4πa³/3. These formulas assume Euclidean geometry. For rolling without slipping, travelled distance equals circumference times the number of revolutions. A completely immersed solid displaces its own volume of liquid, provided no liquid overflows. A displacement vector records signed coordinate changes; the straight-line distance from its initial point is its Euclidean norm. Arc length and chord length are different: a semicircular arc has length πr but its diameter has length 2r. Cutting and rearranging pieces preserves area when interiors do not overlap. Check units, immersion conditions, and whether the question asks for path length or net displacement.''',
'logic':r'''A coding or relationship puzzle is solved by translating each assertion into a precise constraint. A substitution code assigns a fixed digit or symbol to each letter; repeated letters must therefore receive repeated symbols. A letter not present in the example remains unspecified unless an additional rule is stated. In family relationships, replace descriptions one step at a time and identify the speaker before interpreting possessives such as “my”. In assignment puzzles, an exclusion eliminates a possible pairing, and a one-to-one assignment requires each available item to be used once. Distinct people mentioned together are normally distinct participants. Build a small table of possibilities and remove forbidden entries, then verify the surviving assignment against every sentence. Avoid inventing an alphabetic rule from a single example or confusing the person in a photograph with that person's parent.''',
'digits':r'''A base-ten two-digit number with tens digit a and units digit b has numerical value 10a+b, where 1≤a≤9 and 0≤b≤9. Writing ab in a number puzzle denotes this positional value, not the product a times b. The unit digit of an integer is its remainder modulo 10. Congruences can be added and multiplied: replacing each term by a congruent integer preserves the remainder of the final expression. For powers, unit digits often repeat cyclically, and for sums one can also exploit an algebraic identity. The sum of the first n cubes is [n(n+1)/2]² for nonnegative integer n. First express the quantity algebraically, then reduce only as much as needed. Common pitfalls are dropping a place-value factor of ten and reducing an exponent without checking its actual cycle length.'''
}
def add(n,p,o,c,h,s,t,e):w.add(n,'Shared','Aptitude','Easy',p,o,c,h,s,T[t],e)
add(1,r'''Consider the statements: I: All Booklets are Manuals. II: All Manuals are Catalogues. If both are true, which conclusion can conclusively be drawn?''', ['All Manuals are Booklets.','All Catalogues are Booklets.','All Booklets are Catalogues.','All Catalogues are Manuals.'],'C',['Translate each “all” statement into a subset relation.','Chain the two inclusions; their converses need not hold.'],r'''Let B,M,C denote the three sets. The hypotheses give B⊆M and M⊆C, hence B⊆C. Thus every booklet is a catalogue. Reverse inclusions are unsupported: for example B={1}, M={1,2}, C={1,2,3} satisfies both hypotheses and falsifies all three other options.''','sets', [('All roses are flowers and all flowers are plants. What follows?','Writing R⊆F⊆P gives R⊆P, so all roses are plants. Neither converse follows; a grass plant is a counterexample to plants being roses.'),('All squares are rectangles. Must all rectangles be squares?','No. A rectangle of side lengths 2 and 3 belongs to the rectangle set but not the square set, so the reverse inclusion fails.')])
add(2,'The Venn diagram gives sport counts: football only 18, cricket only 24, tennis only 20, football–cricket only 7, football–tennis only 11, cricket–tennis only 8, all three 4. The percentage playing exactly two sports is closest to ____%.',['5','14','28','32'],'C',['Sum only the three pairwise-only regions for the numerator.','The denominator is the sum of all seven disjoint regions.'],'Exactly two sports: 7+11+8=26 players. Total: 18+24+20+7+11+8+4=92. The percentage is 100×26/92=28.2608…%, closest to 28%.','sets',[('A three-set diagram has pairwise-only counts 3,4,5 and total 60. Find the percentage in exactly two sets.','The disjoint exactly-two count is 3+4+5=12. Therefore the percentage is 100×12/60=20%.'),('Two clubs have 30 and 25 members and 10 common members. Find the union and those in exactly one.','Inclusion–exclusion gives union 30+25−10=45. Exactly one is (30−10)+(25−10)=35.')])
add(3,'A company’s value is the total value of its shares. Rakesh owns 2/15 of the shares and sells 1/3 of his shares for Rs. 75,000. What is the total company value then?',['Rs. 15,75,800','Rs. 16,87,500','Rs. 17,75,800','Rs. 18,27,500'],'B',['Find the fraction of the whole company sold.','Multiply 2/15 by 1/3, then divide the sale value by that fraction.'],'The sold shares constitute (2/15)(1/3)=2/45 of the company. Assuming the same share valuation, (2/45)V=75,000. Hence V=75,000×45/2=Rs.16,87,500.','ratio',[('An investor owns 1/4 of a firm and sells half that holding for Rs. 50,000. Find firm value.','The sale is 1/8 of the firm; V/8=50,000 gives V=Rs.400,000.'),('A 3/10 holding is worth Rs.90,000. What is a 1/6 holding worth at the same price?','Total value is 90,000÷(3/10)=300,000. One-sixth is Rs.50,000.')])
add(4,'A car has wheels of diameter 36 cm and travels at 60 km/h. Its wheel rotation rate in RPM is closest to ____.',['884','898','906','986'],'A',['Convert the car speed to centimetres per minute.','Each revolution covers π times the diameter.'],'60 km/h=100,000 cm/min. Wheel circumference is 36π cm. RPM=100,000/(36π)=884.194…; the closest option is 884. The rolling calculation assumes no slipping.','geometry',[('A wheel of diameter 50 cm travels 1 km/min. Find RPM.','Distance is 100,000 cm/min and circumference 50π cm, so RPM=2000/π≈636.62.'),('A wheel of radius 0.3 m turns 500 times/min. Find speed in km/h.','One revolution covers 0.6π m. Speed=300π m/min=18π km/h≈56.55 km/h.')])
add(5,'A cylindrical container of radius 20 cm contains water to height 25 cm. A solid sphere of radius 7 cm is fully immersed. Find the approximate increase in water level.',['1.14 cm','2.28 cm','5.50 cm','7.00 cm'],'A',['The displaced water volume equals the sphere volume.','Divide 4π(7³)/3 by the cylinder’s base area π(20²).'],'The sphere displaces 4π·343/3 cm³. If Δh is the rise, 400πΔh=1372π/3. Thus Δh=1372/1200=1.1433… cm, approximately 1.14 cm. The initial depth 25 cm exceeds the sphere diameter 14 cm, so full immersion is possible.','geometry',[('A sphere of radius 3 cm is immersed in a cylinder of radius 6 cm. Find the rise.','The displaced volume is 36π cm³; base area is 36π cm². Thus Δh=1 cm.'),('A 200 cm³ object is submerged in a tank of base 10 cm by 20 cm. Find rise.','The horizontal cross section is 200 cm². Volume conservation gives 200Δh=200, hence Δh=1 cm.')])
add(6,'Market shares (%) of P,Q,R,S,T are 17,25,24,14,20; annual productions (thousand scooters) are 80,120,110,80,100. Profit is proportional to market share divided by production. X: T and P have the same profit. Y: R has maximum profit. Z: S has minimum profit. Which are correct?',['X and Y','X and Z','Y and Z','Only Z'],'C',['Compute the five share/production ratios.','A common positive proportionality constant does not change their ranking.'],'Ratios are P=17/80=.2125, Q=25/120≈.20833, R=24/110≈.21818, S=14/80=.175, T=20/100=.2. P and T differ, so X is false. R is largest and S smallest, so Y and Z are true.','ratio',[('Scores are proportional to ratios 12/40,15/60,18/50. Which is largest?','The ratios are .30,.25,.36. The third is largest, independent of the common positive multiplier.'),('A and B have shares 20%,30% and production 100,150. Compare profits proportional to share/production.','Both ratios equal .2, so their profits are equal under the stated proportionality.')])
add(7,'Rahul and his father jog on a circular track of radius r (r>2). Rahul completes one round. His father runs half a round and returns to the starting point in a straight line. What is the ratio of Rahul’s distance to his father’s?',[r'πr/(π+2)',r'2π/(π+2)','1','2'],'B',['The father’s straight return joins diametrically opposite points.','His route consists of a semicircular arc plus one diameter.'],r'''Rahul travels 2πr. His father travels πr along the semicircle and 2r along the diameter, total r(π+2). Their ratio is 2πr/[r(π+2)]=2π/(π+2). The radius cancels.''','geometry',[('Compare a full circular lap with a semicircle followed by a diameter for radius 3.','Distances are 6π and 3π+6; their ratio is 2π/(π+2).'),('A quarter-circle arc of radius 4 is followed by its chord. Find total distance.','The arc is 2π. The 90° chord is √(4²+4²)=4√2. Total is 2π+4√2.')])
add(8,'Kavita walks 200 m north, turns 45° right and walks 70 m, then turns 90° right and walks 70 m. Which is closest to the shortest distance from her house?',['296 m','240 m','200 m','223 m'],'D',['Resolve the last two walks into east and north components.','Their north components cancel; their east components add.'],'Use east–north coordinates. The final two displacement vectors are (70/√2,70/√2) and (70/√2,−70/√2). Final position is (70√2,200). Distance=√(9800+40000)=√49800≈223.16 m, closest to 223 m.','geometry',[('Walk 100 m north, then 50 m northeast and 50 m southeast. Find displacement magnitude.','The latter vectors add to (50√2,0), hence distance=√(10000+5000)=50√6≈122.47 m.'),('Walk 3 km east and 4 km north. Find shortest return distance.','The final coordinates are (3,4). The straight distance is √(3²+4²)=5 km, whereas the travelled route was 7 km.')])
add(9,'Initial salaries of John, Riya, Sunil are in ratio 4:3:5. Adding Rs.10,000 to each changes their ratio to 6:5:7. What was Sunil’s initial salary?',['Rs.20,000','Rs.25,000','Rs.30,000','Rs.35,000'],'B',['Write initial salaries as 4k,3k,5k and final salaries as 6t,5t,7t.','Subtract two salary equations to show k=t.'],'Equations 4k+10000=6t and 3k+10000=5t give k=t on subtraction. Then 10000=2k, hence k=5000. Sunil initially earned 5k=Rs.25,000. The third equation also gives 25000+10000=7·5000.','ratio',[('Two salaries in ratio 2:3 become 3:4 after each gains Rs.5000. Find initial salaries.','Let 2k+5000=3t and 3k+5000=4t. Subtraction gives k=t; 5000=k. Salaries were Rs.10,000 and Rs.15,000.'),('Quantities 3k and 5k receive 12 each and become 2:3. Find k.','(3k+12)/(5k+12)=2/3 gives 9k+36=10k+24, so k=12. Initial quantities are 36 and 60.')])
add(10,'Rose, Lotus, Marigold plants have ratio 8:5:7. Their counts increase by 75%,40%,50%, respectively. Find the new ratio in the same order.',['5:3:4','4:2:3','5:4:3','7:4:5'],'B',['Multiply each old ratio entry by its own increase factor.','The factors are 1.75,1.40,1.50.'],'New counts are proportional to 8·1.75=14, 5·1.4=7, 7·1.5=10.5. Dividing all entries by 3.5 yields 4:2:3.','ratio',[('Counts in ratio 2:3 rise by 50% and 100%. Find new ratio.','They become 2·1.5:3·2=3:6=1:2.'),('Counts in ratio 5:8 decrease by 20% and 25%. Find new ratio.','They become 5·.8:8·.75=4:6=2:3.')])
add(11,r'What is the unit digit of 1³+2³+3³+4³+5³+6³+7³+8³+9³?',['0','5','7','9'],'B',['Use the sum-of-cubes identity.','The sum is the square of 1+2+⋯+9.'],'The identity Σ(k³)=[n(n+1)/2]² gives (9·10/2)²=45²=2025. Its units digit is 5.','digits',[('Find the unit digit of 1³+⋯+10³.','The sum is [10·11/2]²=55²=3025, so the units digit is 5.'),('Find the unit digit of 7⁴.','Modulo 10, 7²≡9 and 7⁴≡9²≡1. Indeed 7⁴=2401.')])
add(12,'Suresh asks Ramesh about the person in a photo Ramesh holds. Ramesh says: “I have no brothers or sisters. However, that man’s father is my father’s son.” Who is in the photo?',['Suresh','Ramesh','Ramesh’s son','Ramesh’s cousin'],'C',['Identify “my father’s son” using the no-siblings condition.','That person is the father of the photographed man.'],'Ramesh has no siblings, so his father’s son is Ramesh himself. Therefore the photographed man’s father is Ramesh, and the person in the photo is Ramesh’s son.','logic',[('An only child says, “Her mother is my mother’s daughter.” Who is the woman referred to?','The speaker is the only daughter of her mother, hence “my mother’s daughter” is the speaker. The referenced woman is the speaker’s daughter.'),('A man says, “This child’s father is my brother.” How is the child related to him?','The father is the speaker’s brother, so the child is his nephew or niece; gender must be supplied to select between these terms.')])
add(13,'Three identical semicircles are arranged as in the source diagram: two upper semicircles have a gap 10 between their inner diameter endpoints. The lower semicircle’s left endpoint is 15 right of the leftmost upper endpoint, and its right endpoint is 15 left of the rightmost upper endpoint. What is the common diameter?',[r'5π','20',r'15π/2','25'],'B',['Compare the full horizontal span using the two upper diameters.','The same span equals the lower diameter plus the two outer lengths 15.'],'Let diameter be d. From the upper semicircles the total span is 2d+10. From the lower semicircle and outer segments it is 15+d+15=d+30. Thus 2d+10=d+30 and d=20. Curved lengths are irrelevant to these straight endpoint measurements.','geometry',[('Two equal upper semicircles have a gap 6. A lower equal semicircle has exterior horizontal segments 10 each. Find diameter.','The span equality is 2d+6=d+20, hence d=14.'),('For equal diameters d=12 and upper gap 4, equal lower exterior segments each have length x. Find x.','The total span is 28. Also it is 12+2x, hence 2x=16 and x=8.')])
add(14,'A number is mistakenly divided by 2 instead of multiplied by 2. What percentage change in the result does this mistake cause?',['25%','50%','75%','100%'],'C',['Compare the erroneous result with the intended result.','Use intended result 2x as the percentage denominator.'],'For a nonzero number x, intended result is 2x and erroneous result x/2. The error has magnitude 2x−x/2=3x/2 for positive x. Relative to intended value, this is (3x/2)/(2x)=3/4, a 75% decrease.','ratio',[('A positive number is divided by 3 instead of multiplied by 3. Find percentage decrease.','Correct result is 3x, wrong result x/3. Relative decrease is (3x−x/3)/(3x)=8/9, or 88.888…%.'),('A quantity is multiplied by 4 instead of 5. Find percentage shortfall.','Intended 5x and obtained 4x differ by x. Relative shortfall is x/(5x)=20%.')])
add(15,'The source diagram shows four categories: two large overlapping sets, a small set wholly inside both, and a fourth oval overlapping each without containment. The categories could be:',['Rivers, water bodies, oceans, sources of evaporation','Parliamentarians, celebrities, elected persons, professional politicians','Monkeys, four-legged animals, pet animals, land animals','Furniture, chairs, seats, wooden objects'],'D',['Look for a small category contained in each of two overlapping categories.','Chairs are both furniture and seats; wooden objects can intersect all three.'],'Take chairs as the small circle, furniture and seats as the two large overlapping sets, and wooden objects as the remaining oval. All chairs are furniture and seats. Furniture includes non-seats such as tables; seats can include non-furniture such as rocks. Wooden objects include chairs, non-chair furniture, non-furniture seats, and other objects. Thus these category relations realize the displayed containment and overlap. The other options impose further containments or disjointness inconsistent with the drawing, such as rivers and oceans being disjoint categories inside water bodies.','sets',[('Find categories for a small set contained in two overlapping sets and a fourth intersecting all.','Squares can be the small set, rectangles and rhombi the two overlapping supersets, and red shapes the fourth. Every square is a rectangle and rhombus; non-square rectangles and non-square rhombi exist, and colour crosses all categories.'),('Can dogs, mammals, animals, and pets model two nested sets plus a crossing set?','Dogs⊆mammals⊆animals gives a three-level chain. Pets overlaps these and may include fish, so it crosses mammals but stays within animals. This needs that additional containment and does not fit a diagram where the crossing set extends outside animals.')])
add(16,'In a code, DELTOID is written 3152893. LOTION could be written:',['582986','582981','198396','198392'],'A',['Map the known letters position by position.','Only the code of N is new; compare each option’s first five digits.'],'The mapping is D→3,E→1,L→5,T→2,O→8,I→9. Hence LOTIO must be 58298, and LOTION has form 58298?. Only 582986 has that prefix. N is unspecified by the example, but may consistently be assigned 6.','logic',[('If PLAN is 7246, what could PALL be: 7422 or 7244?','P→7,L→2,A→4,N→6; PALL must be 7422, so the first option fits.'),('If CAT is 351, which could CART be: 3581 or 3815?','The known letters require prefix C,A as 3,5 and final T as 1. Therefore 3581 fits, assigning the previously unspecified R the digit 8.')])
add(17,'For a two-digit number ab, subtract the sum of its digits and divide the result by 9. The result will be:',['always a','always b','neither a nor b','either a or b depending on a+b'],'A',['Write the number as 10a+b.','Subtract a+b before simplifying.'],'The calculation is [(10a+b)−(a+b)]/9=9a/9=a. Therefore the result is always the tens digit a, regardless of b.','digits',[('Apply the rule to 74.','The digit sum is 11. Then (74−11)/9=63/9=7, its tens digit.'),('In base 8 a two-digit number has digits a,b. Subtract its digit sum and divide by 7. Find result.','The number equals 8a+b. Subtraction gives 7a and division by 7 gives a, the leading digit.')])
add(18,'A circle of radius 1 is cut into four quarters and rejoined into the shape shown (four circular arcs forming a curved hourglass outline). What is the shape’s area?',[r'π','1','2','4'],'A',['The question describes a rearrangement of the same four pieces.','Area is conserved when pieces are rejoined without overlapping interiors.'],'The four quarter-circle pieces each have area π·1²/4=π/4. Their rejoined nonoverlapping shape therefore has total area 4(π/4)=π. The change in outer boundary does not change total area.','geometry',[('A circle of radius 2 is cut into eight equal sectors and rearranged without overlap. Find area.','Each sector is (4π)/8=π/2. Eight pieces total 4π, equal to the original circle area.'),('A rectangle 3 by 4 is cut into two triangles and rearranged without overlap. Find area.','The original area is 12. Each diagonal triangle has area 6, so their union after rearrangement still has area 12.')])
add(19,'A trader loses two thirds of her investment one day. The next day she recovers one third of the previous day’s loss. What fraction of her initial investment remains?',[r'1/3',r'2/3',r'2/9',r'5/9'],'D',['Normalize the initial investment to 1.','The recovery is one third of 2/3, not one third of the remaining balance.'],'After losing 2/3 she has 1/3. The recovery is (1/3)(2/3)=2/9. Thus her final holding is 1/3+2/9=3/9+2/9=5/9 of the original.','ratio',[('An investment loses half and then recovers one fourth of that loss. What fraction remains?','The remaining fraction is 1/2+(1/4)(1/2)=1/2+1/8=5/8.'),('A holding loses 40% and recovers half the loss. What percentage of the initial holding remains?','After the loss 60% remains; recovery is half of 40%, namely 20% of initial value. Total is 80%.')])
add(20,'Rahman, George, Vedant wear red, green, violet shirts, one each. Rahman and the person in violet notice no one wears a colour starting with his own initial. Which match is correct?',['Rahman–Violet, George–Red, Vedant–Green','Rahman–Green, George–Violet, Vedant–Red','Rahman–Green, George–Red, Vedant–Violet','Rahman–Red, George–Violet, Vedant–Green'],'B',['Rahman cannot wear red, George cannot wear green, and Vedant cannot wear violet.','Rahman and the violet-shirted person are distinct, so Rahman cannot wear violet either.'],'Rahman is neither red (matching initial) nor violet (he is distinct from that person), hence green. George cannot be green and Vedant cannot be violet. With red and violet remaining, Vedant wears red and George violet. This is option B.','logic',[('Anna, Bea, Cara wear amber, blue, coral, none matching initials. Anna is distinct from the coral wearer. Determine the assignment.','Anna cannot wear amber or coral, so blue. Cara cannot wear coral, so amber. Bea takes coral; all exclusions are satisfied.'),('Three people A,B,C take tasks X,Y,Z. A cannot take X or Z; B cannot take Z. Assign tasks.','A must take Y. The remaining tasks are X,Z; B cannot take Z, so B takes X and C takes Z.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
